/* Section 34.1 part 1, clinical case cards: single best answer. Source: Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry, 12th ed., Chapter 34. */
KS.add("ch34-1", "cases", [
  {"id":"c341-pg01","tag":"Piaget basics","q":"A first-year resident preparing a journal club notes that Piaget held a biology doctorate and wanted to explain how human knowledge itself grows from an innate base. Which term did Piaget mainly use for himself?","choices":["Child psychoanalyst","Genetic epistemologist","Neurophysiologist","Learning theorist","Applied clinical psychologist"],"answer":1,"why":"Piaget described himself first as a genetic epistemologist, studying how abstract thought develops from a biologic or innate substrate (Piaget basics). He was not an applied psychologist."},
  {"id":"c341-pg02","tag":"Piaget basics","q":"Two 8-year-old cousins are tested. One conserves quantity easily; the other still reasons like a younger child. The parents ask whether the second child skipped a stage. Which statement best fits Piaget's model?","choices":["Each child moves through all stages at the same age","Stages reverse once formal operations begin","Stages occur in a fixed order, but the pace varies with endowment and environment","The order of stages depends on the child's culture","Stages can be skipped when the environment is rich"],"answer":2,"why":"Every stage is a prerequisite of later ones, but the rate varies with native endowment and environmental circumstances (Piaget basics)."},
  {"id":"c341-pg03","tag":"Piaget basics","q":"A 5-year-old girl engages in symbolic play, draws pictures and imitates a television character hours after seeing it. According to Table 34-1, which period is she in?","choices":["Preoperations","Formal operations","Sensorimotor","Tertiary circular reaction","Concrete operations"],"answer":0,"why":"Table 34-1 lists deferred imitation, symbolic play, drawing, mental imagery and language for the preoperations subperiod, ages 2 to 7 (Piaget basics)."},
  {"id":"c341-sm01","tag":"Sensorimotor stage","q":"A 7-month-old boy kicks a mobile, watches it move, then kicks again on purpose to make it move. He is starting to anticipate what his actions will do. Which substage fits best?","choices":["Symbolic thought","Primary circular reaction","Inborn reflexes","Tertiary circular reaction","Secondary circular reaction"],"answer":4,"why":"From 5 to 9 months, the secondary circular reaction brings seeking of new stimuli, anticipating consequences and the start of intentional behavior (Table 34-2)."},
  {"id":"c341-sm02","tag":"Sensorimotor stage","q":"A 3-month-old girl sucks her thumb and coordinates her hands and senses, but she does not look for stimuli outside her visual field. Which substage best describes her?","choices":["Primary circular reaction","Tertiary circular reaction","Symbolic thought","Secondary circular reaction","Early object permanence"],"answer":0,"why":"From 2 to 5 months, the primary circular reaction coordinates the body and five senses; reality stays subjective (Table 34-2)."},
  {"id":"c341-sm03","tag":"Sensorimotor stage","q":"A 10-month-old boy laughs at peek-a-boo, copies new gestures and seems only vaguely aware that a hidden toy still exists. Which description fits?","choices":["Fully attained object permanence","Preliminary signs of object permanence","Conservation of substance","Primary circular reaction","Semiotic function"],"answer":1,"why":"Between 9 months and 1 year the infant shows preliminary object permanence, plays peek-a-boo and imitates novel behaviors (Table 34-2)."},
  {"id":"c341-sm04","tag":"Sensorimotor stage","q":"A 22-month-old girl uses a stick to pull a ball toward her and searches the floor when a toy falls from her high chair. She calls the ball by name. Which achievement marks her transition to the next stage?","choices":["Conservation of quantity","Syllogistic reasoning","Attainment of object permanence","Class inclusion","Hypotheticodeductive thinking"],"answer":2,"why":"From 18 months to 2 years the child uses symbols, shows early reasoning and attains object permanence, which marks the move to the preoperational stage (Sensorimotor stage)."},
  {"id":"c341-po01","tag":"Preoperational thought","q":"A 5-year-old boy is asked who is naughtier: a girl who broke one cup on purpose or a boy who broke ten cups by accident. He says the boy, because more cups broke. Which stage does this reasoning reflect?","choices":["Hypotheticodeductive thinking","Concrete operations","Sensorimotor stage","Formal operations","Preoperational thought"],"answer":4,"why":"Preoperational children cannot handle moral dilemmas and judge by the amount of damage (Preoperational thought)."},
  {"id":"c341-po02","tag":"Preoperational thought","q":"A 4-year-old girl insists that thunder makes the lightning happen because they come together. Which form of thinking is this?","choices":["Phenomenalistic causality","Syllogistic reasoning","Animistic thinking","Reversibility","Immanent justice"],"answer":0,"why":"Phenomenalistic causality is magical thinking where things happening together are taken as causing each other (Preoperational thought)."},
  {"id":"c341-po03","tag":"Preoperational thought","q":"A 4-year-old boy says the rain cloud is angry at him and the chair is sad when nobody sits on it. Which form of thinking is this?","choices":["Egocentrism","Immanent justice","Animistic thinking","Seriation","Phenomenalistic causality"],"answer":2,"why":"Animistic thinking gives physical objects and events life-like traits such as feelings and intentions (Preoperational thought)."},
  {"id":"c341-po04","tag":"Preoperational thought","q":"A 4-year-old girl keeps singing loudly after being told her older brother must study for an exam. Her parents think she is being defiant. Which explanation best fits Piaget's view?","choices":["She lacks object permanence","She shows a tertiary circular reaction","Egocentrism prevents her from grasping her brother's viewpoint","She is being deliberately negativistic","She has reached formal operations"],"answer":2,"why":"Preoperational children are egocentric and cannot take another person's role; such behavior is not negativism (Preoperational thought)."},
  {"id":"c341-co01","tag":"Concrete operations","q":"An 8-year-old boy watches a clay ball rolled into a long sausage and says it still has the same amount of clay because nothing was added. Which ability is he showing?","choices":["Immanent justice","Animistic thinking","Conservation","Object permanence","Hypotheticodeductive thinking"],"answer":2,"why":"Conservation is recognizing that an object keeps its quantity despite a change in shape; it marks concrete operations (Concrete operations)."},
  {"id":"c341-co02","tag":"Concrete operations","q":"A 9-year-old girl becomes distressed whenever a game rule is changed and insists every rule be followed exactly. Her teacher asks what the healthiest outcome of this stage would be. Which is it?","choices":["Rejection of all rules in favor of personal choice","Return to egocentric thinking","Strict adherence to every rule without exception","Healthy respect for rules with acceptance of legitimate exceptions","Rules guided only by fear of punishment"],"answer":3,"why":"Excess investment in rules can bring obsessive-compulsive behavior; the most desirable outcome is healthy respect for rules plus understanding of legitimate exceptions (Concrete operations)."},
  {"id":"c341-co03","tag":"Concrete operations","q":"A 10-year-old boy reasons that all dogs are animals and all animals need food, so all dogs need food. He cannot yet reason about purely hypothetical situations. Which stage is he in?","choices":["Preoperational thought","Concrete operations","Secondary circular reaction","Formal operations","Sensorimotor stage"],"answer":1,"why":"Syllogistic reasoning from two premises appears in concrete operations; dealing with future possibilities comes in formal operations (Concrete operations)."},
  {"id":"c341-fo01","tag":"Formal operations","q":"Parents bring their 15-year-old son because he talks at length about ethics, religion and politics and proposes hypotheses to explain world events. He has no hallucinations and functions well. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Early psychotic process","Obsessive-compulsive behavior","Normal formal operational thinking","Concrete operational thinking","Regression to preoperational thought"],"answer":2,"why":"Abstract interests and hypothesis testing are normal in formal operations; such thinking may seem overly abstract without being psychotic (Formal operations)."},
  {"id":"c341-fo02","tag":"Formal operations","q":"A 16-year-old girl, newly able to reflect on her own thinking, believes she can make an exam postponed just by thinking hard about it. Which concept best explains this?","choices":["Lack of object permanence","Return to egocentric thought at a higher level","Immanent justice","Phenomenalistic causality of early childhood","Failure to conserve"],"answer":1,"why":"Adolescents mastering new cognitive tasks may return to egocentric thought at a higher level, such as thinking they can change events by thought alone (Formal operations)."},
  {"id":"c341-fo03","tag":"Formal operations","q":"A 40-year-old man handles concrete daily tasks well but struggles to test hypotheses or reason about abstract probabilities. Which statement about Piaget's stages best fits?","choices":["He must have lost object permanence","Everyone reaches formal operations by age 11","Some people never reach formal operations and remain concrete operational","He is in the preoperational stage","Formal operations is reached only in adulthood"],"answer":2,"why":"Depending on capacity and experience, some never reach formal operational thought and remain concrete for life (Formal operations)."},
  {"id":"c341-pa01","tag":"Piaget in psychiatry","q":"A 14-month-old girl admitted for pneumonia screams whenever her mother leaves the ward. Which measure does the chapter recommend?","choices":["Role-play procedures with dolls","Allow the mother to stay overnight","Limit visits to shorten adjustment","Explain the reasons for admission in detail","Use Socratic questioning"],"answer":1,"why":"Sensorimotor-stage children lack object permanence and have separation anxiety; they do best when mothers can stay overnight (Psychiatric applications)."},
  {"id":"c341-pa02","tag":"Piaget in psychiatry","q":"A 5-year-old boy needs an intravenous line. Which preparation is most helpful for a child at his stage?","choices":["A detailed verbal description of the procedure","A discussion of the abstract reasons for treatment","Role-playing the procedure using dolls and a toy IV set","No preparation at all","A written leaflet about the procedure"],"answer":2,"why":"Preoperational children cannot handle abstractions and benefit more from role-play than from detailed description (Psychiatric applications)."},
  {"id":"c341-pa03","tag":"Piaget in psychiatry","q":"A 6-year-old girl with a fractured arm says she broke it because she had mean thoughts about her sister, and she believes the bone will never heal. Which explanation best fits?","choices":["A delusional disorder","Lack of object permanence","Preoperational limits in cause and effect, conservation and reversibility","Normal formal operational thinking","Concrete operational rigidity about rules"],"answer":2,"why":"Preoperational children may see illness as punishment for bad thoughts and, lacking conservation and reversibility, cannot see how a fractured bone heals (Psychiatric applications)."},
  {"id":"c341-pa04","tag":"Piaget in psychiatry","q":"A 45-year-old woman in the ICU after a crash insists the monitor is watching her angrily and that her bad thoughts caused the accident. She was cognitively typical before. Which explanation does the chapter offer?","choices":["Hypotheticodeductive thinking","Normal formal operational thought","Cognitive regression under stress","Lifelong concrete operational thinking","Arrested sensorimotor development"],"answer":2,"why":"Adults under stress may regress, thinking in preoperational, egocentric and sometimes animistic ways (Psychiatric applications)."},
  {"id":"c341-pa05","tag":"Piaget in psychiatry","q":"A therapist asks a 7-year-old girl to draw one person who feels both happy and angry at the same time, to help her hold mixed feelings toward her father. Whose technique is this, and which operation does it apply?","choices":["Aaron Beck; conservation","Susan Harter; reversibility","Jean Piaget; object permanence","Stanley Greenspan; seriation","Susan Harter; class inclusion"],"answer":4,"why":"Harter's drawing technique carries class inclusion, a concrete operation, into the realm of feelings (Implications for psychotherapy)."},
  {"id":"c341-pa06","tag":"Piaget in psychiatry","q":"A 34-year-old man with alcohol use disorder believes he can only feel happy when drinking. His therapist helps him identify this thought and gather evidence for and against it. What is the critical step after identifying such thoughts?","choices":["Focusing only on overt actions","Exploring only drives and affects","Helping him view the thoughts more objectively rather than accept them as true","Encouraging passive listening to the therapist","Accepting the thoughts as accurate"],"answer":2,"why":"In cognitive therapy the critical process after identification is helping the patient view thoughts objectively, testing them against evidence with active involvement (Implications for psychotherapy)."},
  {"id":"c341-pa07","tag":"Piaget in psychiatry","q":"Through Socratic questioning, a therapist shows a depressed patient that her belief of being unlovable does not fit the facts. She feels unsettled and searches for a better way to understand herself. Which Piagetian concept describes this?","choices":["Seriation","Immanent justice","Object permanence","Equilibration","Phenomenalistic causality"],"answer":3,"why":"Disturbing an inadequate cognitive structure prompts a search for better structures; Piaget called this compensation equilibration (Implications for psychotherapy)."},
  {"id":"c341-pa08","tag":"Piaget in psychiatry","q":"A 22-year-old woman in dialectical behavior therapy lists the events, feelings, thoughts, situational cues and interpersonal factors leading up to self-harm. Which approach to cognition does this chain analysis reflect?","choices":["Sensorimotor learning","Script theory or narrative approaches","Genetic epistemology","Hypotheticodeductive thinking","Classical psychodynamic drive theory"],"answer":1,"why":"Script or narrative approaches are empirically based and domain-specific; DBT chain analysis is the chapter's example (Implications for psychotherapy)."},
  {"id":"c341-dp01","tag":"Developmentally based psychotherapy","q":"A clinician using Stanley Greenspan's model meets a new adult patient. What should the clinician determine first?","choices":["The level of ego or personality development and any deficits or constrictions","The patient's conservation abilities","A chain analysis of recent behavior","The patient's automatic negative thoughts","The patient's reinforcement history"],"answer":0,"why":"Developmentally based psychotherapy begins by determining the level of ego or personality development and deficits or constrictions (Developmentally based psychotherapy)."},
  {"id":"c341-dp02","tag":"Developmentally based psychotherapy","q":"A 30-year-old man cannot read others' nonverbal emotional cues and struggles to hold loving and angry feelings together. In Greenspan's framework, what do these absent capacities indicate?","choices":["Healthy ego development","Normal adult functioning","Emotional disorder, since absent core processes define disorder","Formal operational thinking","A purely cognitive deficit unrelated to personality"],"answer":2,"why":"Greenspan's core processes underlie ego and personality; when present they mean emotional health, when absent emotional disorder (Developmentally based psychotherapy)."},
  {"id":"c341-dp03","tag":"Developmentally based psychotherapy","q":"In a conservation test like those in Figure 34-1, a child sees two equal rows (A), then one row is changed (B). Which answer shows that conservation has been achieved?","choices":["B is a different object from A","B contains more because it looks longer","A and B cannot be compared","B holds an equal quantity to A","B contains less because it looks shorter"],"answer":3,"why":"Per the Figure 34-1 caption, once conservation is achieved the child judges that B holds as much as A (Developmentally based psychotherapy)."},
  {"id":"c341-at01","tag":"Attachment basics","q":"A 29-year-old first-time mother had an emergency cesarean delivery and could not hold her baby for two days. At a 6-week visit she worries that she missed the window to bond. Which statement best reflects the chapter?","choices":["Bonding cannot occur without skin contact in the first hour after birth","Bonding depends only on the number of hours spent together","Bonding failure is certain and the infant will form no attachments","Bonding is the infant's tie to the mother and is fixed at birth","A critical period for bonding after birth is much disputed, and many such mothers bond well"],"answer":4,"why":"Some propose a postpartum critical period, but the idea is much disputed; many mothers without early skin contact show excellent care (Attachment and development)."},
  {"id":"c341-at02","tag":"Attachment basics","q":"A student reviews an experiment in which infant monkeys were raised in isolation from birth. As adults they were withdrawn, could not relate to peers or mate, and neglected their young. Whose work is this?","choices":["René Spitz","Mary Ainsworth","Donald Winnicott","John Bowlby","Harry Harlow"],"answer":4,"why":"Harlow isolated monkeys from birth and showed these emotional and behavioral effects (Harry Harlow)."},
  {"id":"c341-at03","tag":"Attachment basics","q":"A biology student watches newly hatched ducklings follow the first moving figure they see in their first hours of life. Which concept does this illustrate?","choices":["Transitional object","Imprinting","Secure base effect","Signal indicator","Bonding"],"answer":1,"why":"Imprinting is an instinctual attachment system in which stimuli in the first few hours elicit innate behavior during a critical period (Ethologic studies)."},
  {"id":"c341-as01","tag":"Phases of attachment","q":"A 9-month-old girl cries hard when her mother leaves her with a babysitter. When her mother returns, she stops crying and clings to her. Which phase of attachment is she in?","choices":["Attachment in the making","The fourth phase (age 25 months and beyond)","Detachment","Preattachment","Clear-cut attachment"],"answer":4,"why":"Clear-cut attachment (ages 6 through 24 months) features distress on separation and clinging on reunion (Phases of attachment)."},
  {"id":"c341-as02","tag":"Phases of attachment","q":"A 6-week-old boy follows his mother with his eyes across a wide arc and turns and moves rhythmically when she speaks. He shows no distress when others hold him. Which phase of attachment is this?","choices":["Attachment in the making","Preattachment","Clear-cut attachment","Protest","The fourth phase (age 25 months and beyond)"],"answer":1,"why":"Preattachment runs from birth to 8 or 12 weeks, with orienting, 180-degree tracking and rhythmic movement to the mother's voice (Phases of attachment)."},
  {"id":"c341-as03","tag":"Phases of attachment","q":"A 3-year-old boy whose mother is emotionally absent and was abused as a child freezes and behaves strangely when frightened at preschool. Which attachment pattern best fits?","choices":["Insecure-ambivalent","Secure","Anxious-ambivalent adult style","Insecure-disorganized","Insecure-avoidant"],"answer":3,"why":"Insecure-disorganized children have emotionally absent parents with a history of childhood abuse and act bizarrely under threat (Phases of attachment). The chapter credits the type to Ainsworth; Main and Solomon added it."},
  {"id":"c341-as04","tag":"Phases of attachment","q":"A 22-month-old girl with inconsistent parents cannot settle into play at a calm playgroup and keeps clinging to her mother. Which attachment pattern best fits?","choices":["Secure","Insecure-avoidant","Insecure-ambivalent","Avoidant adult style","Insecure-disorganized"],"answer":2,"why":"Insecure-ambivalent children struggle with exploratory play even without danger and cling to their inconsistent parents (Mary Ainsworth)."},
  {"id":"c341-ss01","tag":"Strange Situation","q":"In a research lab, a 14-month-old is first with his parent, then a stranger joins, then the parent leaves him with the stranger, and later he is left completely alone. In which episode of the protocol is the infant alone?","choices":["Episode 7","Episode 2","Episode 5","Episode 4","Episode 3"],"answer":2,"why":"In episode 5 the parent leaves and only the infant remains (Table 34-4)."},
  {"id":"c341-ss02","tag":"Strange Situation","q":"A 2-year-old carries a worn blanket everywhere and uses it to venture into a new playroom while his mother is out. What did Winnicott call such an object?","choices":["Surrogate mother","Object consistency","Transitional object","Secure base effect","Signal indicator"],"answer":2,"why":"Inanimate objects like a blanket act as a secure base; Winnicott called them transitional objects (The Strange Situation)."},
  {"id":"c341-ss03","tag":"Strange Situation","q":"An 8-month-old who is calm with his parents becomes upset and cries when an unfamiliar neighbor leans over his crib. Which phenomenon is this?","choices":["Separation anxiety","Anaclitic depression","Insecure-avoidant attachment","Detachment","Stranger anxiety"],"answer":4,"why":"Stranger anxiety, an anxious response when someone other than the caregiver appears, occurs at about 8 months (Anxiety)."},
  {"id":"c341-ss04","tag":"Strange Situation","q":"A mother tells a pediatrician that whenever her 4-month-old cries she assumes he is hungry, even when he may be in pain or angry. What does the chapter say about this?","choices":["Hunger is the rarest of the three signal types","Responding to cries reinforces and increases crying","Most mothers treat the hunger cry as a sign of any distress","Mothers usually tell the three cries apart reliably","Crying is a minor signal compared with smiling"],"answer":2,"why":"Hunger is the most common signal; some mothers distinguish types, but most read the hunger cry as standing for pain, frustration or anger (Signal indicators)."},
  {"id":"c341-ss05","tag":"Strange Situation","q":"A 71-year-old man whose wife died four months ago comes for a routine visit. According to the chapter, what is he at increased risk of during the year after her death?","choices":["Imprinting","Anaclitic depression","Insecure-disorganized attachment","Stranger anxiety","A physical or mental disorder"],"answer":4,"why":"The death of a spouse raises the chance that the survivor will have a physical or mental disorder in the following year (Losing attachments)."},
  {"id":"c341-ad01","tag":"Attachment disorders","q":"A 4-year-old girl spends 5 months in hospital away from her mother. At first she cried and searched for her, then seemed hopeless; now she barely reacts when her mother visits. Which stage is she in now?","choices":["Despair","Detachment","Attachment in the making","Protest","Clear-cut attachment"],"answer":1,"why":"After separation of more than 3 months, Bowlby described protest, despair and then detachment, in which the child is indifferent on the mother's return (Disorders of attachment)."},
  {"id":"c341-ad02","tag":"Attachment disorders","q":"A 7-month-old who had a normal attachment is suddenly placed in an institution away from his mother. He becomes withdrawn, unresponsive and keeps getting infections, then improves when a consistent surrogate carer is assigned. Which condition best fits?","choices":["Avoidant personality disorder","Anaclitic depression","Insecure-avoidant attachment","Stranger anxiety","Separation anxiety disorder"],"answer":1,"why":"Spitz described anaclitic depression in normally attached infants suddenly separated and institutionalized; they recover with the mother's return or surrogate mothering (Anaclitic depression)."},
  {"id":"c341-ad03","tag":"Attachment disorders","q":"A 6-year-old boy removed from a father who beat him begs to go back home. The foster carer is confused. Which explanation fits the chapter?","choices":["Maltreatment and fear can increase attachment to an abusive parent","He is in the fourth phase of normal attachment","His request proves no abuse took place","He has a secure attachment to his father","Abuse has erased his capacity for attachment"],"answer":0,"why":"Abused children often keep their attachments; rejection or fear of a parent may increase attachment, as dog studies of severe punishment also show (Child maltreatment)."},
  {"id":"c341-ad04","tag":"Attachment disorders","q":"A 3-year-old girl who returns from a long separation turns away from her mother. The mother fears she has been forgotten. What does the chapter say underlies this behavior?","choices":["The child is angry she left and fears she will leave again","The child has forgotten her mother","The child has formed a new imprint","The child is in the preattachment phase","The child has a transitional object instead"],"answer":0,"why":"In detachment the mother is not forgotten; the child resents her for leaving and fears another departure (Disorders of attachment)."},
  {"id":"c341-ap01","tag":"Attachment in psychiatry","q":"A 34-year-old woman in weekly therapy begins to try assertive behaviors she has long avoided, saying she feels safe knowing her therapist is there. Which concept best explains this?","choices":["Imprinting","Stranger anxiety","Secure base effect","Detachment","Anaclitic depression"],"answer":2,"why":"When a patient attaches to the therapist, a secure base effect lets the patient take risks and practice new behavior (Psychiatric applications)."},
  {"id":"c341-ap02","tag":"Attachment in psychiatry","q":"A 38-year-old man has had several short relationships. He feels lonely but pulls away whenever a partner wants more closeness or an argument starts. Which attachment style best fits?","choices":["Insecure-disorganized","Secure","Avoidant","Clear-cut","Anxious-ambivalent"],"answer":2,"why":"Avoidant adults are uninvested in close relationships, often lonely, fear intimacy and withdraw under stress or conflict; break-up rates are high (Relationship disorders)."},
  {"id":"c341-ap03","tag":"Attachment in psychiatry","q":"A 30-year-old woman checks her husband's phone constantly, is preoccupied with him and is consumed by jealousy. Which adult attachment style does the chapter link to this picture?","choices":["Anxious-ambivalent","Secure","Detached","Avoidant","Insecure-avoidant"],"answer":0,"why":"Anxious-ambivalent adults are often preoccupied with partners, intensely jealous and more likely to divorce (Relationship disorders)."},
  {"id":"c341-lb01","tag":"Learning basics","q":"A 9-year-old boy is referred for disrupting his class. The clinician wants to reduce the behavior. According to learning theory, what should the clinician determine first?","choices":["Whether the behavior is a form of sign tracking","If the behavior is respondent or operant","Whether the behavior shows interoceptive conditioning","Whether the behavior reflects a compensatory response","Whether the behavior has already undergone extinction"],"answer":1,"why":"The first step is to classify the acting out as respondent or operant; then the clinician alters what comes before it or what follows it (Basic concepts)."},
  {"id":"c341-lb02","tag":"Learning basics","q":"In a laboratory, a rat presses a lever and receives a food pellet each time. The experimenter stops delivering pellets. What is the most likely result?","choices":["Pressing becomes a respondent behavior","Pressing becomes a compensatory response","Lever pressing increases in vigor","The rat stops pressing the lever","The rat begins salivating to the lever"],"answer":3,"why":"Consequences govern operant behavior in a lawful way; when pellets stop, pressing stops (Operant conditioning)."},
  {"id":"c341-lb03","tag":"Pavlovian conditioning","q":"A psychology student repeats Pavlov's study. After many bell and food pairings, the dog salivates to the bell alone. Which term describes the salivation to the bell?","choices":["Reinforcer","Conditional stimulus","Unconditional response","Unconditional stimulus","Conditional response"],"answer":4,"why":"Salivating to the bell is the learned conditional response (CR); salivation to food itself is the UR (Basic concepts)."},
  {"id":"c341-lb04","tag":"Learning basics","q":"A 45-year-old man starts a new medication. Over weeks he learns which side effects occur and how to take the tablets as instructed despite some reluctance. Which statement best fits this situation?","choices":["Learning can be ignored once a drug is effective","Learning affects only overt behavior, not adherence","Learning applies to psychotherapy but not to drug treatment","Learning processes come into play even with a simple prescription","Learning matters only for respondent behaviors"],"answer":3,"why":"Even prescribing medication brings learning into play: effects, side effects, following directions and overcoming resistance to adherence (Learning theory)."},
  {"id":"c341-lc01","tag":"Learning basics","q":"A 7-year-old girl hits her brother, and her parents respond by removing her access to the television for an hour. Her hitting declines. Which cell of the law of effect does this fit?","choices":["Reward learning","Punishment by a produced bad event","Avoidance","Escape","Omission (time-out from reinforcement)"],"answer":4,"why":"The action prevents a positive event, so behavior declines; this is omission, now widely called time-out from reinforcement (Law of effect, Figure 34-2)."},
  {"id":"c341-lc02","tag":"Learning basics","q":"A 30-year-old woman leaves a crowded room whenever she feels uneasy, and leaving ends the discomfort. Her leaving becomes more frequent. Which process best explains this?","choices":["Positive reinforcement","Omission","Extinction","Punishment","Negative reinforcement"],"answer":4,"why":"An action that ends or prevents a negative event is strengthened: escape or avoidance, which is negative reinforcement (Law of effect, Figure 34-2)."},
  {"id":"c341-lc03","tag":"Pavlovian conditioning","q":"In an animal study, a light reliably comes on just before food is delivered. The animal begins to move toward the light whenever it appears. What is this behavior called?","choices":["Omission","Sign tracking","Escape learning","Extinction","Compensatory responding"],"answer":1,"why":"Approach to a cue that predicts a good US is called sign tracking (Sign tracking, Figure 34-3)."},
  {"id":"c341-lc04","tag":"Learning basics","q":"A therapist treating a learned fear plans to present the feared cue repeatedly without the bad outcome that once followed it, so that the fear declines. Which learning phenomenon is the therapist using?","choices":["Sign tracking","Compensatory conditioning","Extinction","Omission","Reward learning"],"answer":2,"why":"Learned behavior declines when the US once paired with the CS is removed; this extinction has led to effective cognitive-behavioral therapies (Shared variables and extinction)."},
  {"id":"c341-lc05","tag":"Pavlovian conditioning","q":"In an experiment, a tone signals that a mild shock will not occur for the next minute. A frightened rat moves toward the speaker when the tone sounds. Which statement best explains this?","choices":["A CS signalling a drop in a bad event can evoke approach","The tone has become an unconditional stimulus","A CS signalling a rise in a bad event evokes approach","A CS signalling a drop in a good event evokes approach","The tone is acting as a punisher"],"answer":0,"why":"A cue that lowers the probability of an aversive event signals safety and can evoke approach in a frightened organism (Sign tracking, Figure 34-3)."},
  {"id":"c341-pv01","tag":"Conditioned responses","q":"A 58-year-old woman receiving chemotherapy now feels nauseated and refuses a dish she ate shortly before her last infusion. Which mechanism best explains her refusal?","choices":["A compensatory response to the drug","Positive reinforcement of food refusal","A conditioned aversion from pairing the food with illness","Extinction of a flavor preference","Sign tracking to a food cue"],"answer":2,"why":"Chemotherapy can make patients sick and condition a dislike of food eaten shortly before treatment, or of the clinic (Learning to like and dislike foods)."},
  {"id":"c341-pv02","tag":"Conditioned responses","q":"A 25-year-old man who has just finished a large meal walks past a bakery whose smell he has long linked with treats, and he buys and eats a pastry. Which statement best explains this?","choices":["Food cues work only in hungry organisms","The smell has become an unconditional response","His behavior shows extinction of hunger","Food cues can make a satiated person eat more","The smell is acting as a punisher"],"answer":3,"why":"Food cues can lead even a full (satiated) person or animal to eat more (A food cue calls up a whole system)."},
  {"id":"c341-pv03","tag":"Conditioned responses","q":"In a laboratory study, rats repeatedly receive morphine in a distinctive chamber. Later they are placed in the chamber with no drug. What change in pain sensitivity is most likely?","choices":["No change, because no drug was given","Pain sensitivity that matches untreated rats exactly","Complete loss of pain sensation","Decreased pain sensitivity, mimicking morphine","Increased pain sensitivity"],"answer":4,"why":"A morphine-paired cue raises pain sensitivity rather than lowering it: a compensatory response (Drug cues and compensatory responses)."},
  {"id":"c341-pv04","tag":"Conditioned responses","q":"A student reviews studies in which a cue repeatedly preceded alcohol. When the cue is shown alone, what change in body temperature is typically seen?","choices":["A drop followed by fever","No change in body temperature","A drop in body temperature","Loss of temperature regulation","A rise in body temperature"],"answer":4,"why":"Alcohol can lower body temperature, but the conditioned response to an alcohol cue is typically an increase: a compensatory response (Drug cues and compensatory responses)."},
  {"id":"c341-pe01","tag":"Conditioned responses","q":"A 32-year-old man who has used opioids for years, always in his own apartment, injects his usual dose in an unfamiliar motel room and overdoses. Which learning explanation best fits?","choices":["Extinction of his drug-taking behavior","Positive reinforcement from the motel setting","Loss of cue-dependent tolerance in a new environment","Punishment of drug use by the new room","Sign tracking toward the new room"],"answer":2,"why":"Tolerance partly reflects compensatory responses to usual cues; without those cues, tolerance can be lost and overdose becomes more likely (Compensatory responses and drug use)."},
  {"id":"c341-pe02","tag":"Conditioned responses","q":"A 40-year-old woman abstinent from opiates for months returns to the neighborhood where she used. She feels restless and more sensitive to pain, and uses again to make these feelings stop. Which process best describes her renewed use?","choices":["Positive reinforcement","Sign tracking","Omission","Escape learning (negative reinforcement)","Punishment"],"answer":3,"why":"Retaking the drug to get rid of unpleasant compensatory responses to drug cues counts as escape learning, that is, negative reinforcement (Compensatory responses and drug use)."},
  {"id":"c341-pe03","tag":"Conditioned responses","q":"A 27-year-old woman had an unexpected panic attack on a crowded bus. She now feels anxious on buses and becomes alarmed whenever she feels dizzy. Her dizziness is best described as which kind of stimulus?","choices":["A safety signal","An unconditional stimulus","A punisher","A reinforcer","An interoceptive conditional stimulus"],"answer":4,"why":"Early symptoms of an attack, such as dizziness, can become internal (interoceptive) CSs that evoke anxiety or panic (Conditioned fear and anxiety disorders)."},
  {"id":"c341-pe04","tag":"Conditioned responses","q":"A 50-year-old man survived a train derailment that was preceded by a blue flash from the overhead power line. Now police car lights trigger distress, nightmares and reexperiencing. Which disorder does the chapter link to this conditioning?","choices":["Drug dependence","Panic disorder","Specific phobia","Posttraumatic stress disorder","Obesity"],"answer":3,"why":"A CS tied to the trauma's sensory and emotional sides may contribute to the nightmares and reexperiencing characteristic of PTSD (CSs also motivate operant behavior)."},
  {"id":"c341-pe05","tag":"Conditioned responses","q":"In a rabbit study, a brief tone just before a mild shock near the eye produces a well-timed blink. The researchers then lengthen the tone while keeping the same shock. What response is now most likely to dominate?","choices":["Sign tracking toward the tone","Complete extinction of responding","A compensatory rise in pain sensitivity","A stronger eyeblink only","Mainly fear responses"],"answer":4,"why":"When the CS is made longer but the US stays the same, the response shifts mostly to fear (Conditioned fear and anxiety disorders)."},
  {"id":"c341-pe06","tag":"Conditioned responses","q":"A 35-year-old man with an anxiety disorder avoids elevators. His avoidance becomes much more intense whenever he sees reminders of his fear. Which mechanism best explains the increase?","choices":["The fear cue acts as a safety signal","The fear cue produces sign tracking","The fear cue lowers the vigor of avoidance","The fear cue extinguishes avoidance","The fear cue increases the vigor of avoidance behavior"],"answer":4,"why":"An anxiety-provoking cue can make avoidance and escape behaviors more vigorous (CSs also motivate operant behavior)."},
  {"id":"c341-pn01","tag":"Nature of conditioning","q":"A psychology student runs a rat study. A tone has been repeatedly paired with shock. Then a light is added so that tone plus light precede every shock. Later the light is tested alone and produces almost no fear. Which phenomenon explains this result?","choices":["Rapid reacquisition","Second-order conditioning","Spontaneous recovery","Blocking","Sensory preconditioning"],"answer":3,"why":"Because the tone already predicted shock, the light added no new information about the US, so conditioning to it was blocked (Nature of conditioning)."},
  {"id":"c341-pn02","tag":"Nature of conditioning","q":"A 34-year-old woman had her first panic attack while a particular song played in a cafe. She later notices anxiety when she smells the coffee blend that had always been served while that song played, though the coffee was not there during the attack. Which process best explains the anxiety to the coffee smell?","choices":["Blocking","Sensory preconditioning","Counterconditioning","Reinstatement","Conditioned inhibition"],"answer":1,"why":"The smell (B) was paired with the song (A) before A was paired with the panic; B then evokes responding through its link with A, which can feel spontaneous (Nature of conditioning)."},
  {"id":"c341-pn03","tag":"Nature of conditioning","q":"In a laboratory study, a buzzer is presented. Shocks occur on 30 percent of trials with the buzzer and on 30 percent of trials without it. How will the animal most likely treat the buzzer?","choices":["As not a signal for shock","As a signal for shock","As a second-order CS","As a signal for relaxation","As a conditioned inhibitor"],"answer":0,"why":"When the US is equally likely with and without the CS, the CS is not read as predicting the US (Nature of conditioning)."},
  {"id":"c341-pn04","tag":"Nature of conditioning","q":"A young monkey raised in the lab has never seen a snake. It watches another monkey react with terror to a snake and afterward shows fear of snakes itself. It does not learn fear of colorful flowers shown in the same way. Which two concepts are illustrated?","choices":["Observational conditioning and preparedness","Blocking and renewal","Counterconditioning and spontaneous recovery","Sensory preconditioning and rapid reacquisition","Extinction and reinstatement"],"answer":0,"why":"Fear was learned by watching another monkey, and snakes but not flowers readily gained fear, an example of preparedness (Nature of conditioning)."},
  {"id":"c341-pn05","tag":"Nature of conditioning","q":"A 27-year-old man describes how a sudden pounding heartbeat now seems to set off his full panic attacks. Which type of association does the chapter use to explain this?","choices":["Reinstatement","Blocking","Counterconditioning","Conditioned inhibition","Intraevent association"],"answer":4,"why":"The start of a stimulus, here the heart-rate rise as panic begins, becomes a predictor of the full attack (Nature of conditioning)."},
  {"id":"c341-ex01","tag":"Extinction and relapse","q":"A 22-year-old with fear of dogs completes successful exposure therapy. Three months pass without seeing a dog, and on her next encounter she feels marked fear again, in the same park where therapy took place. Which phenomenon best explains this?","choices":["Spontaneous recovery","Rapid reacquisition","Conditioned inhibition","Blocking","Renewal"],"answer":0,"why":"Fear returned because time passed before the CS was met again, in the same context, which is spontaneous recovery (Erasing Pavlovian learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-ex02","tag":"Extinction and relapse","q":"A 40-year-old man's fear of elevators is extinguished during sessions in his therapist's office building. Weeks later, riding an elevator at his workplace, his fear returns strongly. No new frightening events have occurred. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Renewal effect","Rapid reacquisition","Blocking","Reinstatement","Sensory preconditioning"],"answer":0,"why":"The CS was met in a context different from where extinction was learned, so the extinguished fear returned: the renewal effect (Erasing Pavlovian learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-ex03","tag":"Extinction and relapse","q":"A 31-year-old woman whose driving fear had been extinguished is involved in a minor car crash. Her fear returns quickly and strongly on the next drive. Which phenomenon is illustrated?","choices":["Spontaneous recovery","Second-order conditioning","Preparedness","Conditioned inhibition","Rapid reacquisition"],"answer":4,"why":"The CS (driving) was paired with the US again, and the response returned quickly: rapid reacquisition (Erasing Pavlovian learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-ex04","tag":"Extinction and relapse","q":"A therapist treats a 19-year-old with fear of injections by having him practice deep muscle relaxation while viewing images of needles. Which elimination procedure underlies this technique?","choices":["Blocking","Counterconditioning","Sensory preconditioning","Reinstatement","Reconsolidation blockade"],"answer":1,"why":"Pairing a feared CS with relaxation is systematic desensitization, which was inspired by counterconditioning (Erasing Pavlovian learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-ex05","tag":"Extinction and relapse","q":"A resident asks why fear returns after successful extinction. Which explanation does contemporary theory in the chapter favor?","choices":["Extinction adds new learning giving the CS a second meaning that depends on context","The original memory was erased and relearned from scratch","The patient never truly experienced extinction","Extinction strengthens the conditioned inhibitor permanently","The CS has lost all association with the US"],"answer":0,"why":"Extinction adds a second, safe meaning without destroying the original, so like an ambiguous word the response depends on the current context (Erasing Pavlovian learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-cx01","tag":"Context","q":"A 45-year-old woman takes an anxiolytic before each exposure session for her fear of crowds. She does well in sessions but feels intense fear in crowds on days she has not taken the drug. Which concept best explains this?","choices":["Second-order conditioning","Blocking","Preparedness","Rapid reacquisition","State-dependent learning"],"answer":4,"why":"Extinction learned in a drug state is retained best in that same state; testing without the drug renews fear, as in rats extinguished under a benzodiazepine or alcohol (Contextual stimuli)."},
  {"id":"c341-cx02","tag":"Context","q":"A 38-year-old man with an anxiety disorder drinks alcohol to calm himself in social settings and now drinks heavily. According to the chapter, how might drinking keep his anxiety going?","choices":["Alcohol produces sensory preconditioning to social cues","Alcohol speeds extinction so fear never returns","Alcohol acts only as an unconditioned stimulus for fear","Alcohol blocks conditioning to new cues","Relief reinforces drinking while extinction tied to the drug state preserves the anxiety"],"answer":4,"why":"Anxiety relief reinforces drug-taking, and state-dependent extinction may preserve the original anxiety, which may help explain the tie between anxiety disorders and substance abuse (Contextual stimuli)."},
  {"id":"c341-cx03","tag":"Context","q":"In a rat study, fear is reactivated by one or two CS presentations and anisomycin is given; at a later test fear is low. Weeks later, fear of the CS reappears. What does the chapter conclude from such results?","choices":["The memory was permanently erased by protein synthesis blockade","The drug may interfere with retrieval or access rather than erase the memory","Fear returned through second-order conditioning","The original memory was never consolidated","Anisomycin produced blocking of the CS"],"answer":1,"why":"Fear reduced this way can return over time or with reminders, suggesting interference with retrieval or access rather than erasure (Contextual stimuli)."},
  {"id":"c341-cx04","tag":"Context","q":"A 29-year-old with fear of public transport has finished office-based exposure. His therapist wants to lower relapse risk. Which plan best fits the chapter's advice?","choices":["Avoid public transport for several months","Repeat sessions only in the same office","Do exposure on actual buses and give him a reminder card","Stop all exposure once fear is gone in the office","Tell him the fear memory has been erased"],"answer":2,"why":"The chapter suggests extinction exposure in contexts where relapse matters most and retrieval cues such as reminder cards (Contextual stimuli)."},
  {"id":"c341-op01","tag":"Operant learning","q":"On a ward, a 34-year-old man shouts at the nurses' station several times a day, and staff always come over to calm him. The team agrees to stop responding to the shouting and to give him attention during group activities instead. The shouting declines over two weeks. Which process best explains the decline?","choices":["Extinction","Incentive learning","Latent learning","Partial reinforcement extinction effect","Positive contrast"],"answer":0,"why":"Staff attention was the social reinforcer; withdrawing and redirecting it lets the behavior undergo extinction (Operant learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-op02","tag":"Operant learning","q":"A 9-year-old's tantrums stopped after his parents ignored them for a month. After a week at his grandmother's house, the tantrums return briefly on the first evening home. A resident asks what this shows about extinction. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["Extinction does not destroy the original learning","Ignoring a behavior acts as a punisher","Extinction erased the learning, so this is new learning","The tantrums were never reinforced by attention","Tantrums cannot be changed by consequences"],"answer":0,"why":"Operant extinction, like Pavlovian extinction, leaves the original learning intact, so effects such as spontaneous recovery can occur (Operant learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-op03","tag":"Operant learning","q":"A residential program for adolescents gives points for completing chores and attending classes. Points can be exchanged at a store for snacks and outings. Which term best describes this program?","choices":["Token economy","Variable interval schedule","Latent learning","Premack preference test","Negative contrast"],"answer":0,"why":"Token economies use conditioned reinforcers (tokens) that can be traded for valued items, as in schools and institutions (Operant learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-op04","tag":"Operant learning","q":"In a seminar, a student says the reward in operant learning simply stamps the response in. The teacher replies that a modern view instead holds that the organism learns that the action leads to the outcome. Which view is the teacher describing?","choices":["Tolman's latent learning account","Thorndike's stamping-in account","The Premack principle","Skinner's empirical definition","The synthetic view"],"answer":4,"why":"The modern synthetic view holds that the organism links action to outcome, paralleling stimulus and outcome learning in Pavlovian conditioning (Operant learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-py01","tag":"Schedules and choice","q":"A 52-year-old woman spends hours at casino slot machines, pulling the lever rapidly even after long losing streaks. Payouts come after an unpredictable number of plays. Which reinforcement schedule best describes her situation?","choices":["Continuous reinforcement","Variable ratio","Fixed interval","Fixed ratio","Variable interval"],"answer":1,"why":"Slot machines pay after a varying number of responses around an average: a variable ratio schedule, which can generate high response rates (Schedules and choice)."},
  {"id":"c341-py02","tag":"Schedules and choice","q":"A 28-year-old office worker checks his e-mail many times a day. New messages arrive at unpredictable times, so most checks find nothing new. Which reinforcement schedule is maintaining the checking?","choices":["Fixed interval","Continuous reinforcement","Fixed ratio","Variable interval","Variable ratio"],"answer":3,"why":"Rewards (new messages) become available after varying lengths of time, which is a variable interval schedule (Schedules and choice)."},
  {"id":"c341-py03","tag":"Schedules and choice","q":"A 15-year-old has started drinking at weekend parties. His counselor plans to enroll him in a sports team, a music program and a part-time job he enjoys, without directly targeting the drinking. Which principle best supports this plan?","choices":["Quantitative law of effect","Partial reinforcement extinction effect","Incentive learning","Premack deprivation refinement","Negative contrast"],"answer":0,"why":"Raising reinforcement for alternative behaviors (RO) lowers the strength of the target behavior; a reinforcer-rich environment can slow new undesirable behavior (Schedules and choice)."},
  {"id":"c341-py04","tag":"Schedules and choice","q":"A 24-year-old in recovery must choose between using a drug offered right now and going to class to earn credit for a general educational development (GED) certificate. She takes the drug. How is this choice best described?","choices":["Positive contrast","Impulsive choice","Self-control","Learned industriousness","Latent learning"],"answer":1,"why":"Choosing a small immediate reward over a larger delayed one is called impulsive; the delayed reward is discounted (Schedules and choice)."},
  {"id":"c341-py05","tag":"Schedules and choice","q":"In a lab, a pigeon pecks key 1 at a steady rate. The experimenter leaves key 1's reward rate unchanged but greatly increases the reward rate on key 2. What is expected to happen to pecking on key 1?","choices":["It shows positive contrast","It stays exactly the same","It decreases","It increases","It shows spontaneous recovery"],"answer":2,"why":"By the quantitative law of effect, raising RO (reward for other behaviors) weakens B1 even if R1 is unchanged (Schedules and choice)."},
  {"id":"c341-rf01","tag":"Theories of reinforcement","q":"A 7-year-old boy bangs his head, and his mother scolds him each time. When she stops scolding, the head-banging decreases. The mother is surprised. Under Skinner's empirical definition, what was the scolding?","choices":["A punisher","A reinforcer","A neutral event","A discriminative cue","A conditioned stimulus"],"answer":1,"why":"A reinforcer is any consequence shown to strengthen a behavior; since removing the scold reduced the behavior, the scold was the reinforcer (Theories of reinforcement)."},
  {"id":"c341-rf02","tag":"Theories of reinforcement","q":"A therapist observes a 10-year-old during free time: she spends the most time drawing, less on puzzles and the least on homework. The therapist lets her draw only after finishing homework. Which principle is being applied?","choices":["Quantitative law of effect","Partial reinforcement extinction effect","Premack principle","Incentive learning","Positive contrast"],"answer":2,"why":"The Premack principle uses a free preference test; access to a more preferred activity reinforces a less preferred one (Theories of reinforcement)."},
  {"id":"c341-rf03","tag":"Theories of reinforcement","q":"A child's free-time ranking is video games, then reading, then tidying. Under the basic Premack principle, which arrangement would reinforce reading?","choices":["Reading allowed after tidying","Reading allowed after video games","Video games allowed after reading","Tidying allowed after video games","Tidying allowed after reading"],"answer":2,"why":"Only a more preferred activity reinforces a less preferred one; reading is reinforced by video games but not by tidying (Theories of reinforcement)."},
  {"id":"c341-rf04","tag":"Theories of reinforcement","q":"A boy usually prefers playing outside to drawing. After several days in which drawing was held well below its usual level, being allowed to draw now increases how often he finishes his outdoor chores. Which idea explains this?","choices":["Negative contrast","Skinner's empirical definition alone","Partial reinforcement","Refined Premack principle with deprivation","Latent learning"],"answer":3,"why":"The refined principle holds that a less preferred activity can reinforce a more preferred one if it has been suppressed below baseline (Theories of reinforcement)."},
  {"id":"c341-mv01","tag":"Motivation","q":"Rats explore a maze for several days and are removed at the goal without food. When food is then placed at the goal, they immediately run it with very few errors. What does this experiment show?","choices":["Reinforcers are needed for learning","Rats learn only by trial and error with food","Learning occurred without reward, and reward motivated performance","The rats showed negative contrast","Hunger alone drove the rats' errors"],"answer":2,"why":"Tolman's latent learning experiment shows reward is not necessary for learning but gives the organism a reason to act on what it knows (Motivational factors)."},
  {"id":"c341-mv02","tag":"Motivation","q":"Rats trained for weeks with a large food reward are switched to a small reward. They now run more slowly than rats that always received the small reward and appear agitated. Which phenomenon is this?","choices":["Negative contrast","Spontaneous recovery","Latent learning","Learned industriousness","Positive contrast"],"answer":0,"why":"A shift from a high to a lower reward weakens behavior below controls kept on the small reward and involves frustration: negative contrast (Motivational factors)."},
  {"id":"c341-mv03","tag":"Motivation","q":"Two groups of rats learn to press a lever. Group 1 is rewarded on every press; group 2 on about half of presses. When rewards stop entirely, group 2 keeps pressing much longer. What is this called?","choices":["Partial reinforcement extinction effect","Quantitative law of effect","Positive contrast","Premack principle","Incentive learning"],"answer":0,"why":"Actions reinforced intermittently persist longer in extinction than continuously reinforced ones, a paradoxical reward effect (Motivational factors)."},
  {"id":"c341-mv04","tag":"Motivation","q":"A therapist helping a 40-year-old man build new coping skills deliberately rewards him for practicing them even when practice is hard and tiring. Which principle supports this choice?","choices":["Positive contrast","Fixed interval scheduling","Negative contrast","Reinforced effort produces industriousness","Latent learning"],"answer":3,"why":"Effortfulness can be reinforced; reinforcing high effort may make new therapy behaviors more persistent (Motivational factors)."},
  {"id":"c341-mv05","tag":"Motivation","q":"Sated rats learn to press a lever for a novel pellet. Later, hungry rats are tested with no pellets delivered. Only the rats that had earlier eaten the pellet while hungry press at a high rate. Which process explains this difference?","choices":["Partial reinforcement extinction effect","Negative contrast","Latent learning","Positive contrast","Incentive learning"],"answer":4,"why":"In Balleine's 1992 study, hunger boosted pressing only after the rats learned how the pellet affected the hungry state: incentive learning (Motivational factors)."},
  {"id":"c341-mv06","tag":"Motivation","q":"A 31-year-old woman with anxiety is prescribed a helpful medication but rarely takes it during anxious periods. She has only ever taken it when calm. According to incentive learning theory, what might increase her motivation to take it when anxious?","choices":["Removing all other rewards in her life","Having the prescriber scold her for missed doses","Taking it only on a fixed interval","Pairing it with a smaller reward","Experiencing its effect during anxiety"],"answer":4,"why":"Theory suggests an anxious person may need to experience the medication's effect during anxiety before anxiety motivates taking it; this implication is underexplored (Motivational factors)."},
  {"id":"c341-av01","tag":"Avoidance learning","q":"A 29-year-old woman with obsessive-compulsive disorder washes her hands many times a day. She says each wash briefly eases a rising sense of dread. Using two-factor theory, what reinforces the washing?","choices":["Delivery of a food-like reward","Reduction of conditioned anxiety","Spontaneous recovery of fear","Habituation to the dirt cue","Punishment of the washing response"],"answer":1,"why":"Two-factor theory holds that Pavlovian cues arouse fear and the avoidance response is reinforced by reducing that fear; the chapter gives repeated checking or washing as an example (Avoidance learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-av02","tag":"Avoidance learning","q":"A 41-year-old man had panic attacks in shopping malls and now rarely leaves his house. Staying home keeps him calm. Which learning account from the chapter best explains why staying home persists?","choices":["Incentive learning about shopping","Occasion setting by home cues","Escape from fear reinforcing avoidance","Categorization of public places","Habit learning after devaluation"],"answer":2,"why":"The chapter uses the agoraphobic patient who stays home to escape fear of places linked with panic as an example of avoidance reinforced by fear reduction (Avoidance learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-av03","tag":"Avoidance learning","q":"A 19-year-old college student with bulimia reports intense anxiety after eating and relief once she vomits. Her therapist explains the vomiting with two-factor theory. In that account, what role does the meal play?","choices":["A habit stimulus with no emotion","An occasion setter for sucrose","A punisher of eating","An unconditioned reinforcer of vomiting","A cue that evokes learned anxiety"],"answer":4,"why":"The chapter describes eating as evoking learned anxiety, which vomiting then reduces; the meal acts as a Pavlovian cue for fear (Avoidance learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-av04","tag":"Avoidance learning","q":"A student runs two rat groups. Group A must run to another compartment to avoid shock; group B must press a lever to avoid shock. Group A learns quickly and group B struggles. What best explains the difference?","choices":["Group B was reinforced on a ratio schedule","Group A received inescapable shock beforehand","Running is an operant and pressing is a respondent","Running is an SSDR, so Pavlovian learning suffices","Lever pressing cannot be learned by rats"],"answer":3,"why":"Fleeing is a species-specific defensive reaction; with an SSDR only Pavlovian learning is needed, while a lever press needs extra reinforcement by fear reduction (Avoidance learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-av05","tag":"Avoidance learning","q":"In a laboratory class, a rat freezes whenever a tone that predicts shock comes on, and freezing avoids the shock. The instructor asks what controls freezing. Which is the best answer?","choices":["A fixed-interval schedule of escape","Incentive learning about the shock","Its consequences, through fear reduction","Its antecedent, the tone that predicts shock","An S to R habit formed after overtraining"],"answer":3,"why":"Freezing and fleeing are respondents controlled by antecedent CSs that predict shock, not by consequences (Avoidance learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-aw01","tag":"Avoidance learning","q":"Two groups of laboratory animals receive the same shocks; group 1 can end them by a response and group 2 cannot. Later, both must learn a new response to escape shock. What is the expected result?","choices":["Group 2 learns faster because it is sensitized","Group 2 learns more slowly than group 1","Group 1 learns more slowly than group 2","Neither group learns the new response","Both groups learn equally well"],"answer":1,"why":"In learned helplessness, subjects exposed to inescapable shock are poorer at learning a new escape response, while those given escapable shock are normal (Learned helplessness)."},
  {"id":"c341-aw02","tag":"Avoidance learning","q":"A psychology student writes that learned helplessness experiments prove the cause of depression. Which response reflects the chapter's current view?","choices":["Controllability mainly modulates a stressor's stressfulness","The effect occurs only with food reinforcers","Escapable shock produces the helpless pattern","Controllability has no effect on later learning","Helplessness is the established cause of depression"],"answer":0,"why":"The chapter says the finding was at one time treated as a model of depression, but the current view is that controllability mainly modulates stressfulness and negative impact (Learned helplessness)."},
  {"id":"c341-aw03","tag":"Avoidance learning","q":"In an experiment, a pigeon's key is lit for a few seconds before food on each trial. Pecks are not required, yet the bird begins pecking the lit key. What best explains the pecking?","choices":["Negative reinforcement by fear reduction","The lit key is a Pavlovian predictor of food","A habit formed by extensive training","Occasion setting by background noise","Punishment of not pecking"],"answer":1,"why":"The chapter describes key pecking entrained simply by lighting the key before food: the lit key is a Pavlovian predictor of food (Pavlovian control of operant behavior)."},
  {"id":"c341-aw04","tag":"Avoidance learning","q":"An experimenter arranges that any peck on a lit key cancels food that would otherwise come on that trial. What does the chapter report happens?","choices":["The bird freezes as an SSDR","Pecking stops within a few trials","Pecking increases only after devaluation","Pecking continues almost indefinitely on many trials","Pecking shifts to a different key"],"answer":3,"why":"Consequences only weakly control the peck; birds keep pecking almost indefinitely because the lit key still weakly predicts food (Pavlovian control of operant behavior)."},
  {"id":"c341-aw05","tag":"Avoidance learning","q":"A 7-year-old boy is often spanked by his father for jumping on the sofa. He keeps jumping on the sofa when his father is out but now avoids his father. Which explanation fits the chapter?","choices":["The punishment strengthened sofa jumping","He formed an S to R habit for avoiding the sofa","He showed spontaneous recovery of jumping","He learned that his father predicts punishment","He underwent incentive learning about spanking"],"answer":3,"why":"The chapter notes that a punished child may learn to stay away from the punishing parent rather than stop the behavior, a Pavlovian effect (Pavlovian control of operant behavior)."},
  {"id":"c341-sy01","tag":"Instrumental action","q":"Rats learn that a lever press gives pellets and a chain pull gives sucrose. Pellets are then paired with illness. In a test with no reinforcers, the rats chain pull but rarely press the lever. Which association does this result most directly show?","choices":["Background stimulus to fear","Unconditioned stimulus to response","Action to reinforcer","Background stimulus to reinforcer","Stimulus to response habit"],"answer":2,"why":"Reinforcer devaluation shows R to S* learning: the animal knows which action earned which outcome and combines that with its current value (Synthetic view)."},
  {"id":"c341-sy02","tag":"Instrumental action","q":"A student argues that reinforcers simply strengthen whatever action preceded them. Which finding most directly contradicts this view?","choices":["Freezing as an avoidance response","Pigeons pecking a lit key","Learned helplessness after shock","Reinforcer devaluation effect","Spontaneous recovery after extinction"],"answer":3,"why":"The chapter states the devaluation result cannot be explained by the older idea that reinforcers simply stamp in or strengthen actions (Synthetic view)."},
  {"id":"c341-sy03","tag":"Instrumental action","q":"In a devaluation study, rats are made ill after pellets but are never allowed to taste pellets again before the test. The researcher finds the devaluation effect is weak. Which process from the chapter was most likely missing?","choices":["Habit learning","Categorization","Occasion setting","Incentive learning","Negative sign tracking"],"answer":3,"why":"Incentive learning is how the animal learns a reinforcer's value; in devaluation the animal must contact the reinforcer in phase 2 and learn it dislikes it (Synthetic view)."},
  {"id":"c341-hb01","tag":"Instrumental action","q":"In noise, lever presses earn pellets and chain pulls earn sucrose; in light, the relations reverse. Pellets are then devalued. When tested in light, what do the rats mostly do?","choices":["Neither response","Chain pull","Freeze","Lever press","Both equally"],"answer":3,"why":"In light, the chain pull led to pellets, so with pellets devalued rats lever pressed; the cue signaled which action yielded which outcome (Occasion setting)."},
  {"id":"c341-hb02","tag":"Instrumental action","q":"Pigeons learn to sort pictures of cars, chairs, flowers and cats onto four keys. One group sees many examples per category, another sees few. Which outcome does the chapter describe for the many-examples group?","choices":["More errors and worse transfer","No difference from the few-examples group","Fewer errors and better transfer","More errors in training but better transfer","Fewer errors in training and worse transfer"],"answer":3,"why":"More exemplars cause more errors during learning but better transfer to new images (Categorization)."},
  {"id":"c341-hb03","tag":"Habits","q":"Rats are trained on a lever for food for a very long time with consistent reinforcement. The food is then paired with illness, yet the rats keep pressing at a high rate. What best explains this?","choices":["The rats show learned helplessness","Responding is now controlled by an S to R habit","Incentive learning was too strong","The lever became an occasion setter","The food remains a positive reinforcer"],"answer":1,"why":"After extensive, consistent training the devaluation effect no longer occurs, as if the animal responds without recalling the outcome: an S to R habit (Habit learning)."},
  {"id":"c341-hb04","tag":"Habits","q":"A 35-year-old man has performed a checking routine thousands of times and says it now runs automatically. His therapist wonders whether goal-based control is gone for good. Which statement is supported by the chapter?","choices":["Habits can sometimes return to action to reinforcer control","Habits are controlled only by their consequences","Habits permanently destroy action to outcome learning","Habits cannot develop in clinical behaviors","Habits form after only a few repetitions"],"answer":0,"why":"The chapter reports that habit dominance does not destroy cognitive learning, and under some conditions a habit can be brought back under action to reinforcer control (Habit learning)."}
]);
